# pricing.ine — a three-tier pricing page written with ONLY the current
# dictionary vocabulary (page, header, logo, navigation, container, banner,
# card-group, card, section, button, footer, title/text).
#
# Evidence-collection experiment (PLAN mission 54): feed the BLOCKED `link`
# mission (61) with real-program data. Every line the vocabulary could not
# guide is flagged with `vocab gap:` and listed in .agent/NOTES.md
# ("## Mission 54"). Nothing here uses an undeclared kind.

make page
page title "Ine Cloud — Pricing"

make header
header inside page
make logo
logo text "Ine Cloud"
logo inside header
make navigation
navigation inside header
# vocab gap: navigation has no `contains` — the dictionary declares no
# link/button membership and no destination for any nav item, so the nav
# bar renders as an empty strip with no clickable targets (dictionary-demo
# notes the same).

make content container
content inside page

make pitch banner
pitch text "Pick the plan that fits."
pitch inside content

# Three tiers. `make 3 card` supplies card-1..card-3; the group places them.
make plans card-group
plans inside content
make 3 card
group tiers = card-1 card-2 card-3
tiers inside plans

# Each tier is one card. A pricing card really wants a price value and a
# list of features, but a card carries only `title` + one `text` string and
# no numeric/boolean values, so the list is crammed into one text line.
card-1 title "Starter"
card-1 text "Free — highlights: 3 projects, community support, 1 GB."
card-2 title "Pro"
card-2 text "$29/month — highlights: unlimited projects, priority support, 100 GB."
card-3 title "Enterprise"
card-3 text "Custom — highlights: SSO, SLA, dedicated support."
# vocab gap: no per-tier line item list, no price field, no boolean flags
# (e.g. "includes SSO") — each card holds one undifferentiated text string.

# Per-tier "Choose plan" button: buttons belong inside sections only, and
# sections belong inside the page — a button cannot sit inside a pricing
# card, so each tier has no call-to-action of its own. One shared section
# below is the only place a CTA can structurally live.
# vocab gap: `button inside card` / a section inside a card-group are both
# undeclared containment pairs.
make choose section
choose title "Ready to start?"
choose inside page
choose text "Free plan, no credit card required."

# The CTA cannot navigate anywhere: there is no link kind, no property that
# carries a URL, and no click behavior. The closest reachable statement is
# text that merely *mentions* a destination.
make pick button
pick text "View all plans at example.com/pricing"
pick inside choose
# vocab gap: this is display text, not a link — nothing makes the text
# clickable, nothing gives it a destination.

make footer
footer text "© 2026 Ine Cloud — pricing"
footer inside page

say page title